Here is how data is typically structured inside these files, using a standard syntax framework found in SIL App Builders and localized data formats.
Because these files are formatted as structured plain text, you can open and edit them easily using any basic code editor like VS Code, Notepad++, or Sublime Text.
## 1. Inside the .qst (Questions File)
The question file uses identifiers to group questions, individual multiple-choice selections, and target answers together.


[Question 1]
Type = MultipleChoice
Text = Which file extension is the master project file for SIL App Builder?
A = .cls
B = .qst
C = .sil
D = .ch
Correct = C
Hint = It matches the acronym of the organization that makes the software.

[Question 2]
Type = MultipleChoice
Text = What platform is a .cls file used to style layout configurations for?
A = Worksheet
B = Database
C = Audio Track
D = Font Engine
Correct = A

## 2. Inside the script.ch (Chapter / Script File)
Depending on your exact project version, the chapter text maps out the core reading content or localized structural text that the worksheet calls upon.

\c 1
\p This is the opening line of text defined inside your chapter file.
\p Each marker indicates a new paragraph block rendered sequentially on screen.
\p When a user finishes reading this chapter, the system references the layout rules in your worksheet.cls file to build the matching quiz questions.

